// also a cool thing i think we need to have especially for machine scopes
// is to allow to set how many time a certain label can run
// ex: that machine will apply some of the need it updates
machine<int32> counter {
    @init[1] {
        local := 10;
        this {
            count := 0;
        }
        goto -> @step;
    }

    @step[max_limit + 1] {
        count = count + 1;
        if (count >= max_limit) {
            print(@init::local);
            goto -> @finish;
        }
        @init::local += 10;
        goto -> @step;
    }

    @finish {
        leave;
    }

    handle -> {
        fn call(max_limit: int32) -> int32 {
            this.max_limit := max_limit;
            goto -> @init;
        }
        fn leave() -> int32 {
            return this.count;
        }
    }
}
// so as you see by adding the usage in the [] 
// so for init if we try to run it again that will give an error also we can set a default value
// for ex any label with the name init or finish will have a one usage only
// for the step it will decrease the value of the usage i put [max_limit + 1]
// but i think there is a better way to done it 
// i think it will help some  but after thinking 
// i think we can leave it as it is and leave it for the dev but for init or finish 
// we will apply the usage rule

// we need to improve the handles but first we need to categorize them 
// the best way to do so first we need to categorize the scopes (types) also 
// we will categorize them as blocks and blueprints (objects def)
// block is the most basic excitable block but it have some unique treats
// block it one of the ter 1 scope 
// so block can define other ter 1 scopes (class , struct , blueprint , block) in it but other blocks cannot
// so (fn , method , micro  , machine) are ter 2 ; it cannot have ter one define it only block but they well consider a ter 2 
// so every ter 1 defined inside any thing else consider ter 2
// blueprint is the basic object def so you can have it in ter 2 scope
// first the micro don't have a handle also we will apply that for method 
// so for micro to handle it you 
// only need to call it in a scope that have a handle for the ops done in the micro
// as you know we inject the micro in where it been called
// for method what happen in side it only will be handled by the parent scope
// so we need to set some behaver for the ops like (leave , yield , return ,..) in what it will do if the parent scope is the global scope
// also i think it will be cool if we have some default handle we can override
// but first we need to decide what work with what
// (fn , machine , (Objects def but for the method and fn it have)) they will have ones for the ops like (leave , yield , return ,..) 
// (machine , Objects def)  will have a call handle
// the call handle work when we use () after the name and it works after we define a new object
// i mean if we call the name of the type of the object (the blueprint itself (class, struct)) (the object def)
//so if we have :
/*
class X {};
X(); // that call the contractor not the call handle
X x = new X();
x(); // that call the call handle
*/
// but for machine we done have a contractor so we only have call 
// objects def have drop handle and blocks can have is_done handle
// in the default is_done handle we will drop every thing in the block (fn ,..) so the is_done happen after the return 
// i think to allow to only make some changes in the is_done handle we need to have a built-in drop() micro 
// that we can call it to make the is_done work as default so it will delete every thing in the fn memo in this call 
// is_done return void f course 
// drop() will generate a del for every var in the fn needed to be deleted 
// i think it better to make drop() used default at the end in every scope i mean the is_done don't need to drop() every thing
// but also it need to restore some thing to the initialized values so i think we only will make drop() happen under the hood in every in of a scope
// after return but before is_done 
// after thinking we don't need is_done handle that much , we only need the flag is_done 
// so actually we need to set the drop rules to manage the data dropped
// so the drop() micro is like a default drop() to automatically del the ptrs or call the drop handle from the Objects in the scope

// now we need to improve the yield handle or we will make a default one for it like what we will do with other handles
// so what the yield handle will do is handle when the user use yield 
// if he write only yield; that will freeze the whole scope
// but if he write yield var; that will only freeze that var and the yield handle will take name type with the default value this
// i mean the yield will take a ref from were it used with the ref for the whole scope or only for the var
// if we call the block that have yield in it add it yielded it will return the value that return from the yield handle if there one
// and freeze some var values as it is
// and then it will go to the first of the block again, but it will not reset the freezed vars
// but i think to make freezing vars are easy we will make a built-in freeze() micro 
// that will take the name ref for the var and freezed and we will make it work under the hood
// ex:
machine counter2 {
    @init {
        this {
            count := 0;
        }
        goto -> @step;
    }

    @step {
        count = count + 1;
        if (count >= max_limit) {
            goto -> @finish;
        }
        yield count;
        goto -> @step;
    }

    @finish {
        leave;
    }

    handle -> {
        fn call(max_limit: int32) -> int32 {
            this.max_limit := max_limit;
            goto -> @init;
        }
        fn yield(v : name) -> name{
            freeze(v);
            return v;
        }
        fn leave() -> int32 {
            return this.count;
        }
    }
}
